## AI Development Workflow

This project follows a staged AI-assisted development workflow. The canonical reference is:

`docs/workflow/development-workflow/README.md`

Repository-specific workflow providers live in `.ai-dev-workflow.yaml`. Today, `review.on_draft.runner` is consumed by the Step 7a internal review gate protocol, and `review.on_draft.github` / `review.on_ready.github` are consumed by `pr-review-loop.sh` (Step 7); other sections are advisory until more tooling adopts them. Legacy review keys remain accepted for one transition release.

### Stages and protocols

| Stage | Protocol |
|---|---|
| Write spec | `docs/workflow/development-workflow/protocols/01-generate-spec-protocol.md` |
| Write plan | `docs/workflow/development-workflow/protocols/02-generate-implementation-plan-protocol.md` |
| Implement | `docs/workflow/development-workflow/protocols/03-implement-development-protocol.md` |
| Review gate | `REVIEW.md` and the compatibility wrappers under `docs/workflow/development-workflow/protocols/` |
| Batch orchestrate | `docs/workflow/development-workflow/protocols/90-batch-orchestrate-work-protocol.md` |
| Orchestrate one item | `docs/workflow/development-workflow/protocols/91-orchestrate-work-protocol.md` |

### Key rules

- Always read the spec and plan before implementing (Refactor items have no spec — read the plan and work item brief instead)
- Continue through the review gate, PR readiness, and CI unless the protocol surfaces a real human decision
- Always add or update a `changelog.d/` fragment before opening a PR (except spec/plan-only PRs; for fixes to unreleased work, update the existing fragment instead of adding a duplicate; in parallel batches, each PR keeps its own fragment); **hotfix exception**: `hotfix/*` PRs write a new versioned section (e.g., `[1.0.1] - YYYY-MM-DD`) directly below `## [Unreleased]` in `CHANGELOG.md` (above all prior versioned sections) — hotfixes patch released code and are released immediately on merge
- No `git push --force`, `reset --hard`, or rebase on shared branches without explicit human approval
- Humans merge PRs — agents open them
- Cursor Task/subagent runs consume model quota according to the subagent's
  configured `model` in `.cursor/agents/*.md`, not just the Composer model.
  Use `/run-item` and the configured subagents for normal workflow runs; override
  a subagent model only for a specific complexity, cost, quota, or failover
  reason and restore the tier intent afterward. See
  `docs/workflow/development-workflow/agent-model-config.md`.

### Cursor dispatch profile

This rule applies only in a Cursor environment; other runners are unchanged. Before any mutating bounded-command action (`/run-item`, `/run-items`, `/run-epic`) or before a `/run-work` scan reports, declare the dispatch profile in force -- `cursor-native-handoff`, `cursor-parent-orchestrated`, or `cursor-inline-fallback` -- naming the accountable orchestration role for the active layer (Portfolio Orchestrator, Epic Runner, or Work Item Runner), per the canonical, normative `docs/workflow/development-workflow/integrations/cursor-dispatch-profiles.md`.

Compactly: initial handoff is evaluated first; onward-handoff capability is evaluated only once initial handoff is confirmed -- a profile decision never evaluates onward-handoff capability before initial handoff is confirmed. Once initial handoff is confirmed, onward-handoff capability that cannot be confirmed is treated as unavailable and declares `cursor-parent-orchestrated` as the conservative default; separately, initial handoff availability that itself cannot be confirmed is treated the same as no handoff of any kind, declares `cursor-inline-fallback`, and stays read-only -- a later confirmation never upgrades a run in place. A declaration states personally accountable (absorbed), handed off intact, or observing for the role; observing is valid only at a read-only checkpoint, and the required posture follows the current checkpoint. Named stop conditions (exact strings): `dispatch_profile_declaration_missing` -- affected work item: the branch, PR, or development-folder path (or, for a pre-branch `/run-items` explicit-list stop, `explicit_list_invocation_targets=<t1>,<t2>,...`, rendered verbatim and percent-encoded per the canonical doc); human unblocking action: the stopped run is not resumed or corrected in place -- start a fresh invocation with a valid profile, a named accountable role, a posture valid for the checkpoint, and, on a fact mismatch, the profile the known facts assign. `dispatch_handoff_unavailable` -- human unblocking action: move to an environment where initial handoff is confirmed available and re-run, or explicitly accept the read-only result (for a parent-orchestrated stage loss, confirm the specific stage role is reachable first). `missing_required_secret_or_permission` (reused) -- grant the identified credential/permission and re-run the same delegated action, or reassign to the same stage role or explicitly accept the action does not proceed; the absorbing context never performs it inline; this path never extends to a harness tool or local file-path permission denial. A reachable stage role's harness tool or local-path permission denial is not a named stop condition -- observably similar only to `SUBAGENT_PERMISSION_DENIAL` (Work Item Runner to Portfolio Orchestrator only) -- and is Out of Scope, tracked as #1746. An invalid profile, an invalid accountable role, an invalid posture, and a coarse-fact mismatch (more permissive or less permissive than the assigned outcome) are each a missing declaration; this coarse check does not govern the mid-run recovery transitions, which remain valid re-declarations.

